~  PLERROR.STR        04  May  1985
~
~     This file contains all the error messages used by PROMlink.  Each error
~ message is preceded by a '|', an optional number of dashes (-), the error
~ number, a period and a code specifying which programmer and Pak the error
~ message is valid on.  These error messages need not be in order provided 
~ the '|' label as described above accompanies it.  The code describing the
~ valid programmers and paks is described below:
~
~     Model 19      = 800
~     Model 22      = 400
~     Model 60	    =1000
~     Model 120/121 = 200
~     Model 29      = 100
~     GangPak       =  10
~     LogicPak      =   8
~     MOSPak        =   4
~     UniPak        =   2
~     Gang Module   =   1
~
~     These numbers are additive and represented in hexadecimal.  The error 
~ message itself is divided into two strings.  The first line of the error
~ message is the string printed when the error occurs.  The rest of the 
~ lines of the error message are printed out if the user requests more help
~  for the error.
~     As if not already obvious, lines beginning with the '~' character 
~ are considered to be comments and are ignored by PROMlink.
~ 
|----------E18.900
Illegal RAM page
      You are using a programmer with extended RAM capabilities with
      a Pak not equipped to handle the extra RAM.

      Until you can get your Pak software updated, you must use less
      of your programmer RAM for device-related operations with this Pak.
|----------E20.1F1F
Non-blank device
      The programmer has determined that your device has bits in it
      that are already programmed.

      Replace the device or continue if this is acceptable.
|----------E21.1F1F
Illegal bit error
      The programmer has determined that your device has programmed
      bits in it which are not contained in the programmer RAM.

      A programming operation with this device and RAM data will not
      succeed.
|----------E22.1D1F
Programming error
      The programmer was unable to program a bit in your device.

      Discard the device or attempt to program it again.  If this error
      persists, check the calibration of your programming electronics.
|----------E22.200
Failed EEPROM test
      The device failed to erase or program completely during cyclic
      testing.

      Replace device if faulty.
|----------E23.1D16
First pass verify error
      The device failed to compare with programmer RAM during a
      parametric two pass verify operation.
|----------E24.1D16
Second pass verify error
      The device failed to compare with programmer RAM during a
      parametric two pass verify operation.
|----------E25.408
No socket adapter installed
      A device related operation was attempted with no socket adapter
      installed on the programmer.

      Install or re-seat the correct socket adapter and try again.
|----------E25.900
No programming Pak
      A device related operation was attempted with no programming Pak
      installed in the programmer.

      Install or re-seat the correct socket adapter and try again.
|----------E25.200
Bad Family/Pinout code
      An incorrect device type or family/pinout code was entered.

      Check your device type and programming electronics to be sure
      they are compatible and try again.
|----------E26.1400
Wrong socket adapter
      The wrong socket adapter is installed for the device you have
      selected.

      Install the correct socket adapter and try again.
|----------E26.900
Program Pak reset
      The current device related operation was aborted due to a reset
      condition in the program Pak.  This is usually caused by an over-
      current condition due to a bad or poorly inserted device.

      Replace the device and try again.
|----------E26.600
Device in wrong socket
      A device is placed in a socket on the programmer which would cause
      the result of the current operation to be ambiguous.

      Remove the device from the socket and try again.
|----------E27.F16
Insufficient RAM
      Current operation requires more RAM than is available in your
      programmer at this time.

      Check your operation boundaries.
|----------E28.200
Block Size too high
      The block size you have set is either larger than your available
      RAM or (for device related operations) larger than the device
      type you have selected.
|----------E29.200
Verify error
      The device failed to compare with programmer RAM during a verify
      operation.  If this error was received while programming a device,
      it is an indication that the device did not program correctly.
|---------E30.40E
Bad Family/Pinout code
      An incorrect device type or family/pinout code was entered.

      Check your device type and programming electronics to be sure
      they are compatible and try again.
|----------E30.200
Odd load error
      Loaded odd number of devices for 16-bit mode.

      Load set with even number of devices.
|----------E31.141F
Over-current error
      An over-current condition was detected by the programming
      electronics.

      This usually indicates a faulty or poorly installed device.
|----------E31.200
Device not tri-state
      The device's data lines did not go into a high impedance state
      when the part was deselected.

      This usually indicates a bad or poorly inserted device.
|----------E32.41F
Backwards device
      Likely, the device is inserted upside-down in the programmer
      socket.

      Check the device and try again.
|----------E32.1000
Backwards device/Vcc Overcurrent
      (1)  Device plugged in backward; turn it around.
      (2)  Vcc overcurrent, probably caused by a faulty device.

      Check the device and try again.
|----------E32.200
Device not enabled
      All data pins of the device in the programmer socket do not
      drive adequately when the device is enabled.

      This is usually caused by a bad or poorly inserted device.
|----------E33.200
Data line shorts
      A short was detected on the data lines of the device in the
      programmer socket.

      This is usually caused by a faulty or improperly inserted device.
|----------E34.141E
Bad Family/Pinout code
      An incorrect device type or family/pinout code was entered.

      Check your device type and programming electronics to be sure
      they are compatible and try again.
|----------E34.001
Bad characterizer
      The operation aborted due to a faulty or poorly seated
      characterizer.

      Check the characterizer and try again.
|----------E34.200
Address line short
      A short was detected on the address lines of the device in the
      programmer socket.

      This is usually caused by a faulty or improperly inserted device.
|----------E35.017
Faulty chip select
      Data was present from the device while it was supposed to be
      deselected.

      This is usually caused by a faulty or improperly inserted device.
|----------E35.400
Device error
      A program operation was attempted on either a faulty device or
      an empty socket.

      Check the device and try again.
|----------E35.008
Source equation translation error
      An error exists in your source equations.

      Check the equation buffer to find the error.
|----------E35.200
Device not tri-state
      The device's data lines did not go into a high impedance state
      when the part was deselected.

      This usually indicates a faulty or poorly inserted device.
|----------E36.008
Begin RAM address not zero
      The Begin RAM address cannot be set to anything except zero when
      using this Pak.

      Use the Set operations boundaries command to reset the Begin RAM
      address back to zero and try the operation again.
|----------E36.200
Bad insertion
      A device is either mis-registered, upside-down or otherwise poorly
      inserted in the programmer socket.

      Insert it correctly and try the operation again.
|----------E37.006
Socketing error
      Current was being drawn from a socket that should have been empty
      during the attempted operation.

      Check to be sure that no devices are inserted in the wrong sockets,
      and try again.
|----------E37.008
Invalid device-related operation
      This is usually caused by an attempt to do a device-related
      operation with the design adapter in place.
|----------E37.200
Byte count too low
      An operation was attempted with a Byte count that could cause
      damage to the current device type or an ambiguous result.

      Usually, this is caused by trying to program fewer than ten bytes
      in a 3 voltage part.
|----------E38.141F
Illegal operation attempted during calibration
      An improper calibration step was requested or a device related
      operation was attempted while the programmer was in calibration mode.
|----------E38.200
Over-current error
      An over-current condition was detected by the programming
      electronics.

      This is usually due to a faulty or poorly installed device.
|----------E39.1000
Security fuse blown
      An attempt was made to program a device with the security fuse blown.

      Error occurs for MMI devices only.  Device can not be programmed
      again if security fuse blown.
|----------E3A.1000
No functional data
      A load or a test operation was attempted with test mode (function E6)
      selected for structured vector and fingerprint test only, and no
      vectors are loaded and the number of fingerprint cycles are zero.

      Select an alternate test mode or resolve the undefined vectors or
      cycle count.
|----------E3B.1000
Continuity error
      A bad connection was detected between the programmer and the device.
      This is usually caused by device misalignment or poor contact with
      the handler socket.
      Check the position of the device in the handler and the connections
      between the programmer and the device.
|----------E41.1F1F
Framing error
      The serial interface detected a start bit, but the stop bit was
      incorrectly positioned.

      This is usually caused by an improper baud rate or stop bit setting.
|----------E42.1F1F
Receiver over-run error
      The serial interface received characters when the programmer was
      unable to service them.

      Be sure that the proper handshaking is being used or connected.
|----------E43.900
Framing and over-run errors
      The serial interface received characters it was unable to service
      with the stop bit incorrectly positioned.

      Check the baud rates and stop bit selection.
|----------E43.200
Parity error
      The serial interface has determined that a character was received
      which had incorrect parity.

      Check your connections and your parity setting and try again.
|----------E44.200
Unidentifiable interrupt
      Processor IRQ line was pulled for no apparent reason.

      Ignore this error.  If it persists, contact your local
      service center.
|----------E46.1F1F
I/O timeout
      No usable characters were received from the serial interface
      for about 25 seconds or no characters could be transmitted due
      to the state of the handshake lines for about 25 seconds.

      Check all connections and try again.
|----------E47.900
Faulty ACIA chip
      The programmer has determined that its serial interface chip is faulty.

      Contact your local service center.
|----------E47.200
Port 1 FIFO over-flow
      Characters destined to be sent out the main serial port over-
      flowed the internal buffer due to lack of handshaking on the
      second serial port and/or excessive baud rate on the second
      serial port with respect to the main port.
|----------E48.1F00
I/O over-run
      The serial port input buffer received too many characters after
      the handshake line informed the sending device to stop.

      Make sure the handshake lines are connected and operative.
|----------E49.200
Port 2 FIFO over-flow
      Characters destined to be sent out the second serial port over-
      flowed the internal buffer due to lack of handshaking on the
      main serial port and/or excessive baud rate on the main serial
      port with respect to the second port.
|----------E50.900
I/O error
      Because of address errors or an invalid format, no data was
      transferred during input.

      Check that your translator format is correct.
|----------E51.200
I/O format error
      The programmer received an invalid character in the address field.

      Check your connections and data source and try again.
|----------E52.1F1F
I/O verify fail
      The data from the serial port did not match the data in programmer
      RAM.
|----------E53.200
I/O format error
      The number of input records did not equal the record count
      (in MOS Technology format).

      Check your connections and data source and try again.
|----------E54.200
I/O format error
      The record type was in error (Intel Intellec 8/MDS format).
      Check your connections and data source and try again.
|----------E55.200
I/O format error
      The sumcheck received by the programmer does not match with
      the programmer's calculated sumcheck.

      Check your connections and data source and try again.
|----------E56.200
I/O format error
      The combination of the I/O offset address and the address of
      the incoming formatted data would cause data to be stored at
      an address where there is no programmer RAM.
|----------E57.200
I/O format error
      A non-hexadecimal character was received where a hexadecimal
      character was expected.

      Check your connections and data source and try again.
|----------E58.200
I/O format error
      The address check was in error (Signetics Twin and Tektronix
